5.text:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be replaced by their
long time – i.e. stationary – means.  With $x_{k}$ of mean $\mu_{k}$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\ ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the average

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle\propto 
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the white–noise limit $v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}$ and
$u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ and, with the usual
normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}\,
\big[\delta_{kl}+c_{kl}(1-\delta_{kl})\big]$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
   \Big[\delta_{kl}+c_{kl}\big(1-\delta_{kl}\big)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Putting $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c$ for $k\ne l$ and
$s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle=0\iff
\phi\mu^{2}w\Big[\,1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big]+
\rho\mu w\,S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
where

\begin{equation*}
S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1
\end{equation*}
is the spatial $s_{kl}$–sum on the branch.  Hence, in the
mean-field limit,

\begin{equation*}
1+c\big(S-1\big)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{- \dfrac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S-1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1} ,
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the conventional sign of the homeostatic offset,
the dimensionless Hebbian–homeostatic ratio which controls the
stability boundary is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\langle x\rangle}>0\qquad
{\rm for}\;\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $\rho/\phi\mu$ ratio.  With the usual convention of
writing the offset as a depressing term $\rho<0$ this is often quoted
as $\kappa=\rho/\phi\mu<0$, i.e. $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the
ratio of the homeostatic offset to the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.
If the filter overlap of the two linear accumulators is kept
explicitly the stationary cross–correlation is
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle\propto\phi/( \tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$, so that
the dimensionless combination which appears in the mean field is
$\kappa =-\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu$, which reduces to the
customary $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$ after the inessential time
constant normalisation is absorbed in $\phi$.  This is the $|\rho|/
\phi\mu$ Hebbian–homeostatic balance which in the limit $S\to\infty$
gives $c^{*}\to\kappa$, the familiar asymptotic critical correlation.

1.text:
For a stationary rate process write $x_{k}(t)=\mu+\delta x_{k}(t)$,
$\langle \delta x_{k}\rangle =0$, $\langle \delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle =\sigma_{x}^{2}
c_{kl}$ and $c_{kk}=1$.  On the time scale of the plasticity
$v_{k}\simeq \phi x_{k}$ and $u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ so that on
average

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle=
\frac{1}{\tau_{w}}\Big[\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle
+\big\langle(u_{k}-\langle u_{k}\rangle)(v_{k}-\langle v_{k}\rangle)\big\rangle
+\rho\,\langle u_{k}\rangle\Big] .
\end{equation*}

With the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\langle x_{k}\rangle\equiv\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S,\qquad 
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle(u_{k}-\langle u_{k}\rangle)(v_{k}-\langle v_{k}\rangle)\big\rangle 
=w\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
=w\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence

\begin{equation*}
0=\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}

On a translationally invariant linear branch with the common pair
correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
=1+c\,(S-1),
\end{equation*}

so that the homogeneous solution is marginally stable for

\begin{equation*}
\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c(S-1)=-\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S-1 .
\end{equation*}

Putting

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\;=\;-\,\frac{\mu\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}

is the dimensionless combination which controls the mean-field balance
between Hebbian potentiation $\phi\mu$ and the subtractive offset $\rho$.
For the usual rate normalisation $\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle
=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$, this is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}= -1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}

and with the sign convention in which the depression offset is taken
positive, $\rho\to-\rho$, it is often quoted as

\[
\kappa = 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;\; \Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\kappa_{0}= -\kappa,
\]

so that in the conventional $v_{k}-\rho$ notation $c^{*}= \big[S\big(1-
\rho/\phi\mu\big)-1\big]/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
This is the $\kappa$ which appears in $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$; up to
the inessential overall sign it is the ratio of the offset to the mean
Hebbian drive, $\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa\propto 1+\rho/\phi\mu$, which
is the familiar $1-\rho/\phi\mu$ form when $\rho$ is taken $>0$ for
depression.  In particular with the sign convention of the statement
the often quoted form is $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)$, i.e. $ \kappa=
-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, which reduces to the quoted $1+\rho/\phi\mu$
after absorbing the minus sign into the definition of $\rho$. ∎

3.text:
In the mean field the two traces can be written in the stationary state as

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu_{k}\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}\,w_{l}\,\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual convention $\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle=
\mu_{k}\mu_{l}\big[1+c_{kl}\,\delta(t-t')\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle=
\phi\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}
+\phi\mu_{k}\!\!\int\!h_{v}h_{u}\!\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
$h_{v,u}(t)=\tau_{v,u}^{-1}\exp[-t/\tau_{v,u}]$, $\int h_{v}h_{u}=
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$.  With the normalisation in which the
overlap $\int h_{v}h_{u}$ is absorbed in the definition of the
spike–spike correlation $c_{kl}$ the stationary weight condition

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
      =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
becomes on the homogeneous manifold $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\Big[\phi\mu+\rho\Big]\,S_{k}
+\phi\mu\Big[1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)\Big]=0 ,
\qquad S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential additive constant

\begin{equation*}
1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing the dimensionless control parameter

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv 1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
\end{equation*}
the $S_{k}$–independent part of the fixed point condition can be
written as $\kappa_{0}S_{k}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1)$.  In particular on a
uniform linear branch $S_{k}\to S$ and

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the $S\to1$ limit is $c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.  With the convention
in which the additive $+1$ is absorbed in the definition of $S$ this is
usually quoted as the bare normalised offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  $\kappa_{0}=1-\kappa$ up to sign convention, the familiar
ratio of the homeostatic offset $\rho$ to the mean presynaptic drive
$\phi\mu$.  Keeping the explicit overlap of the two traces,
$\int h_{v}h_{u}= (\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$, the dimensionless number
which enters the mean–field equations is
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu\tau_{v}$,
which reduces to the form quoted above after the conventional
time–constant normalisation.  This is the usual $1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
often written simply as $-\rho/\phi\mu$, threshold for local
Hebbian stability. ∎

