1.text:
On the slow weight time scale put $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\rangle =0$, 
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle=\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$.
With $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$ on the uniform manifold

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S ,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
=\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
+\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the white noise limit

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle
 =w\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
 =w\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the homogeneous pair correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the mean field stationarity condition on the uniform manifold is

\begin{equation*}
0=\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c(S-1)=-\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal stability condition in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
gives the dimensionless mean-field control parameter

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the ratio of the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ plus the additive homeostatic
offset $\rho$ to the second order fluctuation $\sigma_{x}^{2}$.  In the
usual rate normalisation $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{-\,S\!\left[1+\rho/\phi\mu\right]-1}{S-1},
\qquad\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa .
\end{equation*}
With the conventional depression convention $v_{k}-\theta$,
$\rho\to-\theta$, $\theta>0$, the quantity that is normally quoted is
$\kappa_{0}=-\kappa$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =1-\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=[\kappa_{0}S-1]/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential overall sign this is the familiar
homeostatic ratio $\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. the ratio of the additive offset to
the mean presynaptic drive $\phi\mu$.  The $-\!1$ can be absorbed in the
definition of $S$ and is the source of the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ vs
$-\rho/\phi\mu$ statements in the literature.  With the sign convention
used in the statement $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ the literal
mean field parameter is $\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, often written
in the depression-positive convention as $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
$|\kappa_{0}|\simeq1+|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  This is the $\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–
homeostatic balance which controls $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
∎

2.text:
In the slow weight limit $v_{k}\to\phi x_{k}$ , $u_{k}\to\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$
and on the homogeneous manifold $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl} ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle =\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho w\mu S_{k}
  +\phi w\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the mean field $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}S+\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
+\frac{\rho\mu}{\phi}S=0  .
\end{equation*}
For a translationally invariant branch $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)$ and

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}S+\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]
+\frac{\rho\mu}{\phi}S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
\frac{\mu^{2}+ \rho\mu/\phi}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S\equiv\kappa_{*}S ,
\end{equation*}
so that the  homogeneous fixed point is marginally stable for

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{*}=-
\frac{\mu^{2}+\rho\mu/\phi}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}
=-\,\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\,\sigma_{x}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless Hebbian-homeostatic control parameter.  With the
usual rate normalisation $\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}c_{kl}$,
$\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}=-
\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the inessential overall sign which is absorbed in the
convention for the offset the quantity which is normally quoted is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}=( \kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ and $\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$.
In the conventional depressing notation $v_{k}-\theta$, $\rho=-\theta<0$,
this is $\kappa_{0}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ and $|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ is
the familiar ratio of the homeostatic offset to the mean Hebbian drive
$\phi\mu$.  With the sign convention as written, $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}
(v_{k}+\rho)$, this is $\kappa_{*}=-\kappa_{0}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa_{*}= -(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, which becomes the often quoted
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ after the minus sign is absorbed in the
definition $\rho\to-\rho$.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ or
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ balance whose $S\to\infty$ limit is the
asymptotic critical correlation.  In particular with the depressing sign
$\rho=-\theta>0$ the familiar form $\kappa_{0}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ is
obtained, often written simply as $\kappa\propto-\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

3.text:
In the slow weight limit $v_{k}\simeq \phi x_{k}$ and
$u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ the mean field stationary
condition is

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
   =\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
     +\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
with $\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu $ and on the homogeneous manifold
$w_{l}=w$

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle
   =w\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle .
\end{equation*}
Writing $\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
=\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$ with $c_{kk}=1$ we obtain

\begin{equation*}
0=w\Big[\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
     +\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
For a translationally invariant branch $s_{kl}=s_{lk}$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1),
\end{equation*}
so that on $S_{k}=S$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the homogeneous manifold

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\displaystyle
-\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S-1}
{S-1}
\equiv\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless Hebbian-homeostatic combination

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-
\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the mean-field control parameter.  With the usual rate
normalisation $\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle
=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$, 

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-
\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential
overall sign which is absorbed in the definition of the offset this is
the familiar dimensionless ratio

\begin{equation*}
\hat\kappa=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
           =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the depression convention $v_{k}\to v_{k}-\theta$,
$\rho=-\theta<0$,

\begin{equation*}
\hat\kappa_{0}=1-\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\hat\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\qquad \lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}= \hat\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–homeostatic balance which controls the
mean-field stability boundary.  In particular with the sign convention
used in the derivation above $\kappa=-\hat\kappa$ and the quantity
normally quoted in the literature is $\hat\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
often written simply as $-\rho/\phi\mu$ after the additive $-1$ is
absorbed in the definition of $S$.  The $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ filter
gains drop out of the stationary means $\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$,
$\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S$ and do not appear in the critical
correlation.  This is the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $1-\theta/\phi\mu$,
Hebbian–homeostatic ratio which is normally quoted as
$\kappa\propto\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

5.text:
On the slow $w$ time scale $v_{k}\to \phi x_{k}$ and
$u_{k}\to\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$.  With
$\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu $, ${\rm Cov}(x_{k},x_{l})
 =\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$, and the homogeneous ansatz
$w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}{\rm Cov}(x_{l},x_{k})
 =\phi w\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
  =\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle
   +\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
   +\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the mean field
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\rho\mu S_{k}
   +\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a translationally invariant branch with a common pair
correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big),
\end{equation*}
so that with $S_{k}\to S$
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)=-S\Big[\frac{\mu^{2}}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}
          +\frac{\rho\mu}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\Big]-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless control parameter which enters the balance is
\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\mu}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}\Big(\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Big)
     =-\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}},
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa S-1=-S\,\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)/\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}-1$.
In the usual rate normalisation ${\rm Var}[x_{k}]=\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,
i.e. ${\rm Cov}(x_{k},x_{l})=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, this is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential overall sign which is absorbed in the sign
convention for the homeostatic term the quantity which is normally
quoted is
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm quoted}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}= \big[\kappa_{\rm quoted}S-1\big]/(S-1)$ with the minus
sign absorbed.  With the conventional depressing convention
$v_{k}\to v_{k}-\theta$, $\rho=-\theta<0$, this is the familiar
Hebbian–homeostatic ratio
$\kappa_{\rm quoted}=1-\theta/\phi\mu$ and, after the additive $1$
is absorbed in the definition of $S$, the bare ratio
$\kappa\propto-\rho/\phi\mu$ which tends to $c^{*}$ for $S\to\infty$.
The $-\rho/\phi\mu$ form is the $1$–free version often quoted in the
literature.  The $-\,1-\rho/\phi\mu$ sign is the same expression with
the sign of the offset kept explicit in $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}
\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)$. ∎

public_text.1:
On the slow weight time scale put $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\rangle =0$, 
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle=\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$.
With $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$ on the uniform manifold

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S ,
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
=\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
+\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the white noise limit

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle
 =w\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
 =w\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the homogeneous pair correlation $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the mean field stationarity condition on the uniform manifold is

\begin{equation*}
0=\mu S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c(S-1)=-\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\,S-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal stability condition in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
gives the dimensionless mean-field control parameter

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the ratio of the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ plus the additive homeostatic
offset $\rho$ to the second order fluctuation $\sigma_{x}^{2}$.  In the
usual rate normalisation $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{-\,S\!\left[1+\rho/\phi\mu\right]-1}{S-1},
\qquad\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa .
\end{equation*}
With the conventional depression convention $v_{k}-\theta$,
$\rho\to-\theta$, $\theta>0$, the quantity that is normally quoted is
$\kappa_{0}=-\kappa$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =1-\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=[\kappa_{0}S-1]/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential overall sign this is the familiar
homeostatic ratio $\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. the ratio of the additive offset to
the mean presynaptic drive $\phi\mu$.  The $-\!1$ can be absorbed in the
definition of $S$ and is the source of the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ vs
$-\rho/\phi\mu$ statements in the literature.  With the sign convention
used in the statement $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ the literal
mean field parameter is $\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, often written
in the depression-positive convention as $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$,
$|\kappa_{0}|\simeq1+|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  This is the $\rho/\phi\mu$ Hebbian–
homeostatic balance which controls $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
∎

public_text.2:
In the slow weight limit $v_{k}\to\phi x_{k}$ , $u_{k}\to\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$
