5.text:
On the $w$ time scale the two first order filters can be taken in the
quasi static limit

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)\simeq\phi x_{k}(t),\qquad 
u_{k}(t)\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}(t) ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the homogeneous manifold $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with $x_{k}=\mu+\delta x_{k}$,
$\langle\delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle=\sigma_{x}^{2}c_{kl}$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\big\rangle
 =w\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle\delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\rangle
 =w\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl} .
\end{equation*}
The mean field stationarity condition
$\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle\propto\langle u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)\rangle=0$
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
0=\langle u_{k}\rangle\langle v_{k}\rangle+
\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle+\langle\delta u_{k}\delta v_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $S_{k}=S$

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and on a translationally invariant branch $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\rho\mu S+\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c(S-1)=-S\,
\frac{\mu(\phi\mu+\rho)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal stability condition in the canonical form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless mean field control parameter which enters the
balance is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\mu}{\sigma_{x}^{2}}\Big(\mu+\frac{\rho}{\phi}\Big)
     =-\,\frac{\mu\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
the ratio of the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$ plus the additive
homeostatic offset $\rho$ to the second order fluctuation
$\sigma_{x}^{2}$.

In the usual rate normalisation ${\rm Var}[x_{k}]=\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu^{2}$,
${\rm Cov}(x_{k},x_{l})=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, this is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{*}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S)=\frac{\kappa_{*}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{*}\; .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential overall sign which is absorbed in the convention
$v_{k}\to v_{k}-\theta$, $\rho\to-\rho$, the quantity normally quoted in
the literature is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=-\kappa_{*}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}=[\kappa_{0}S-1]/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  With the conventional depressing convention
$\rho=-\theta<0$, $v_{k}\to v_{k}-\theta$, this is the familiar
Hebbian–homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1-\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu}\approx-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
