correlation.  The $-\,1$ can be absorbed in the definition of $S$ and is
the source of the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ versus $-\rho/\phi\mu$ statements in
the literature.  The stationary means $\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$,
$\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S$ are independent of $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ so
that with the white–noise rate normalisation the filter gains drop out
of the stationary mean field balance, the quoted $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ ratio
being the controlling dimensionless number.  With the sign convention as
written $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ this is the literal
$\kappa=-\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa=-\mu(\phi\mu+\rho)/
\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}$, normally quoted as $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

